  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM MDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nA ridge of high pressure over the southwest combined with an upper \nlow off the Pacific coast will support the flow of monsoon moisture \ninto Southeast Idaho over the next several days in southwest flow \naloft. Shower and thunderstorm coverage will become more widespread \nagain this afternoon and evening as an embedded shortwave works \nthrough. The Storm Prediction Center has included almost the \nentirety of the CWA in a Marginal Risk of severe weather today. The \nstorm environment becomes more favorable heading into the afternoon \nthrough mid-evening hours, with models indicating 0-6km shear \nincreasing to 40 to 50 knots across portions of the CWA. The threat \nof gusty winds increases as well, with RRFS DCAPE values ranging \naround 1000 to 1400 J/kg. Thunderstorms will be capable of \noutflows ranging 50 to 60 mph. Otherwise, moderate to heavy rain\nwill be possible with storms with models indicating \nprecipitable water ranging 0.70\" to 1.4\" today. Outside of \nthunderstorms, winds will remain fairly weak today. Temperatures\ntoday will run 5 to 10 degrees cooler than yesterday's record \ntemperatures.\n\nThe main upper level low pressure system will continue to track \nnorthward into Canada throughout the day Sunday, resulting in less \nwidespread storm coverage on Sunday with activity more limited to \nthe higher terrain. On the other hand, winds will increase outside \nof storms with gusts across the Snake River Plain, Arco Desert, and \nportions of the central mountains ranging 30 to 40 mph. The \nsuppression of the ridge will mean a continuation of gradually \ncooling temperatures through Monday before the ridge attempts to \nrebound. During this time, temperatures will briefly warm by several \ndegrees, but ensembles show the next PacNW low reaching the coast \nand flattening the ridge again come mid-week which will take our \ntemperatures back into seasonable territory or just below for the \nlatter half of next week. Southwest flow aloft will keep enough \nmonsoon moisture lifting northward to support daily chances of \nshowers and thunderstorms throughout next week.",
